26	
27	% ============================================================
28	\begin{abstract}
29	% ============================================================
30	Test-time scaling via multi-sample generation improves LLM performance on reasoning tasks, but its effectiveness for structured information extraction (IE) remains unclear.
31	We systematically evaluate five confidence signals across four families for quality estimation and best-of-$N$ selection on three NER datasets (CoNLL-2003, SciERC, Few-NERD) and one RE dataset (SciERC), using Qwen3-8B, Qwen3-4B, and LLaMA-3.1-8B.
32	While confidence signals reliably predict output quality (AUROC up to 0.82 on CoNLL, Spearman $\rho$ up to 0.45), they \emph{universally fail} to select better candidates than greedy ($T{=}0$) decoding.
33	We diagnose two failure mechanisms: (1)~\textbf{instance degeneracy}---21--58\% of instances produce identical outputs across all $N$ samples, eliminating selection headroom; and (2)~\textbf{logprob compression}---the only per-sample signal exhibits within-instance score ranges of merely 0.028--0.041 nats, rendering candidate discrimination impossible.
34	These mechanisms are unified by a \textbf{capability trap}: as model capability increases, outputs converge, degeneracy rises, and selection headroom vanishes.
35	We confirm this pattern across three NER datasets ($R^2{=}0.94$ for the F1--degeneracy relationship), two model scales, and a LoRA rank ablation that rules out fine-tuning intensity as a confound.
36	Our results establish that $T{=}0$ greedy decoding dominates all multi-sample strategies for structured IE, and that closing the gap requires fundamentally different approaches such as trained verifiers.
37	\end{abstract}
